# =============================================================================
# Acropora OS — Production Environment Variables
# =============================================================================
#
# This file documents the environment variables the running application reads
# in production. It is a reference template, not something to `cp` and fill in
# blindly: in Coolify, set these as the application's environment variables
# (grouped per docs/COOLIFY.md), not as a committed .env file. Never commit a
# real .env.production to git.
#
# Tags used below:
#   [REQUIRED]   the app will misbehave or refuse to boot without this
#   [REQUIRED*]  required only if the named feature/integration is enabled
#   [OPTIONAL]   has a safe built-in default; only set to override it
#   [SECRET]     a credential — put it in Coolify's secret store, never in
#                logs, screenshots, or a committed file
#   [DEV ONLY]   must NEVER be set in a production environment
#   [RESERVED]   present for forward-compatibility; no code path reads it yet
#
# HOW COMPLETE THIS FILE IS, AS OF 2026-09-04, AND WHY IT IS WORTH SAYING.
#
# This header used to claim it held EVERY variable the application reads,
# "verified against the source". That was measured and was not true: nine
# Medusa variables stood in neither template, and two whole scheduler families
# stood only in the development one. Both gaps are now closed here.
#
# AS OF 2026-09-04 NOTHING IS MISSING: the ISMERT_HIANY list in
# apps/api/src/common/env-template-coverage.spec.ts is EMPTY. That guard walks
# the source for four different access shapes and fails the build if a variable
# outside its exception lists is undocumented, so a new gap cannot open
# unnoticed.
#
# THIS SENTENCE IS ONLY WORTH READING BECAUSE A MACHINE KEEPS IT TRUE. The
# header used to promise the same thing on a human's word, and it was wrong by
# thirty-two variables.
#
# A header that promises completeness is worse than one that names its own
# gap: the first stops the reader from looking, the second tells them where.
#
# See also:
#   docs/DEPLOYMENT.md  — full production deployment process, including the
#                          "first production boot" checklist these variables
#                          feed into.
#   docs/COOLIFY.md      — exactly how these map onto Coolify's environment
#                          variable groups per application.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core runtime
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [REQUIRED] Must be exactly "production". A large amount of production-only
# behavior is gated on this: prisma.config.ts refuses to silently fall back
# to a local DB connection string, packages/database/prisma/seed.ts refuses
# to run at all, apps/api/src/auth/auth.service.ts refuses the development
# mock login, and the UNAS connection startup validator (see below) becomes
# active.
NODE_ENV=production

# [REQUIRED] Postgres connection string for the api service. Must be reachable
# from the api container's network (Coolify-managed Postgres resource or an
# external instance) — never point this at a database exposed on a public
# port. Recommend sizing the connection pool explicitly for a small Hetzner
# VM, e.g. append `&connection_limit=10&pool_timeout=10` — see
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md's Prisma section for sizing guidance.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database>?schema=public&connection_limit=10

# [REQUIRED] Redis connection string, used for health checks today and
# available for future session storage. Must not be reachable from outside
# the internal Docker network.
REDIS_URL=redis://<host>:6379

# [REQUIRED] Port the NestJS api process listens on inside its container.
# Matches the Dockerfile's EXPOSE 3001 and HEALTHCHECK; only change this if
# you also change the Dockerfile/Coolify port mapping together.
PORT=3001

# [REQUIRED] Public origin of the web app. Used by the api for its single
# allowed CORS origin (apps/api/src/main.ts) — must be the real production
# domain, e.g. https://erp.acropora.hu. Never set to "*".
WEB_URL=https://erp.example.com

# [REQUIRED] Internal address the web app's server-side rewrite
# (apps/web/next.config.ts, /api/* -> API_URL) uses to reach the api service.
# Inside Coolify's internal Docker network this is normally the api service's
# internal hostname and port, e.g. http://acropora-api:3001 — NOT the public
# domain, and not proxied through TLS internally.
API_URL=http://acropora-api:3001

# [OPTIONAL] A partnereszközök QR-kódjának mobil deep link alapja. Az
# alapértelmezés ugyanez; explicit értékkel látható marad a deploy-szerződés.
# A token automatikusan /<uuid> útvonalként kerül a végére.
ASSET_QR_BASE_URL=acropora-os://assets/scan

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UNAS integration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# UNAS is the webshop/catalog system Acropora OS mirrors product and order
# data from (read-only for products; see docs/ARCHITECTURE.md). Two things
# below are load-bearing for whether the API can even start in production —
# read the note before UNAS_CREDENTIAL_ACTIVE_KEY_VERSION carefully.

# [REQUIRED*, SECRET] The UNAS shop API key. Required whenever the
# UnasConnectionSetting row's credentialMode is ENV_FALLBACK — which is what
# every fresh database gets by default (the migration that creates this table
# inserts that row directly). In other words: unless someone has explicitly
# switched credential storage to the database-encrypted mode via the admin
# UI, this variable is effectively required in production.
UNAS_API_KEY=

# [REQUIRED in production, regardless of the above] apps/api's
# UnasConnectionStartupValidator runs only when NODE_ENV=production, and it
# unconditionally validates the active UNAS master key — even if the
# connection is using ENV_FALLBACK mode and never actually decrypts anything
# with it. Miss this and the API will fail its onModuleInit() and crash-loop
# on every single boot, not just when UNAS features are used. Generate the
# key with: openssl rand -base64 32
UNAS_CREDENTIAL_ACTIVE_KEY_VERSION=1
# [REQUIRED in production, SECRET] Exactly 32 random bytes, base64-encoded,
# matching the active version number above (the variable name's suffix,
# "V1", must match UNAS_CREDENTIAL_ACTIVE_KEY_VERSION). Keep old *_V<n>
# variables around, unset, if you ever rotate — the code looks them up
# dynamically by version to support in-flight rotation.
UNAS_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY_V1=

# [OPTIONAL] Default already points at UNAS's production API; only override
# for a UNAS sandbox/staging shop.
UNAS_API_URL=https://api.unas.eu/shop

# [OPTIONAL] Feature flags for the in-process background sync schedulers.
# Each *_ENABLED defaults to disabled (safe) if unset. Reminder from
# docs/PRODUCTION-DEPLOYMENT-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md: these run inside the
# same process as the HTTP server and are NOT safe to duplicate across
# multiple api replicas — keep api at one replica while any of these are on.
UNAS_PRODUCT_SYNC_ENABLED=false
UNAS_PRODUCT_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
UNAS_PRODUCT_SYNC_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=30
UNAS_CUSTOMER_SYNC_ENABLED=false
UNAS_CUSTOMER_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
UNAS_CUSTOMER_SYNC_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=30
# This note used to say these were missing from the development .env.example.
# Re-measured 2026-09-04: all three stand in both templates, so the gap is
# closed and the note would now send the reader looking for a hole that is not
# there. (It also said "these two variables" while listing three.)
UNAS_ORDER_SYNC_ENABLED=false
UNAS_ORDER_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5
UNAS_ORDER_SYNC_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=30

# [OPTIONAL] The stock outbox worker. Same single-replica caveat as the
# schedulers above. Every bound below is read from the code, not chosen here:
# an out-of-range value throws AT STARTUP rather than silently falling back.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_ENABLED=false
# Seconds between rounds. 1..3600.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_INTERVAL_SECONDS=15
# Seconds before the first round after startup. 0..3600.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=30
# Outbox rows per round. 1..200.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_BATCH_SIZE=20
# How long a claimed row stays claimed. 10..3600. Comfortably longer than
# setStock's own worst case, so a merely-slow worker is not mistaken for a
# crashed one.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_LEASE_SECONDS=120
# Retries before a row is given up on. 1..50.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8
# First retry delay. 1..3600.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_BASE_BACKOFF_SECONDS=30
# Retry ceiling. THE LOWER BOUND IS NOT A CONSTANT: it is whatever
# UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_BASE_BACKOFF_SECONDS holds, so the ceiling can never
# be below the first delay. Setting the two independently is how you get a
# startup failure. Upper bound 86400.
UNAS_STOCK_SYNC_WORKER_MAX_BACKOFF_SECONDS=1800

# [OPTIONAL] Reconciliation for orders deleted on the UNAS side. Same
# single-replica caveat; same startup-throw on out-of-range values.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_ENABLED=false
# Minutes between rounds. 1..1440.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_INTERVAL_MINUTES=30
# Seconds before the first round after startup. 0..3600.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=60
# Orders re-checked per round. 1..100.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_BATCH_SIZE=10
# How long a claimed batch stays claimed. 10..3600.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_LEASE_SECONDS=120
# How long after an order was last checked it becomes eligible again.
# 1..720 (thirty days).
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_RECHECK_HOURS=24
# First retry delay, in seconds. 1..3600.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_BASE_BACKOFF_SECONDS=60
# Retry ceiling, in MINUTES — note the different unit from the base delay
# above, which is in seconds. 1..1440.
UNAS_ORDER_DELETION_RECONCILIATION_MAX_BACKOFF_MINUTES=60

# [OPTIONAL] The shop's own time zone, used to read UNAS's local timestamps.
# An IANA zone name, not a number. Unset means Europe/Budapest.
UNAS_SHOP_TIME_ZONE=Europe/Budapest

# [RESERVED, not yet wired to any code path] Present for the webhook-based
# alternative to polling, once/if that ships. Safe to leave unset.
UNAS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NAV Online Számla (tax authority) integration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Used for company-data lookups (queryTaxpayer) and domestic incoming-invoice
# queries (queryInvoiceDigest/queryInvoiceData). Unlike UNAS above, these
# Credentials can be entered on Settings / NAV and are then stored as one
# encrypted database payload. The NAV_* credential variables below remain the
# first-deploy/env fallback until that UI save succeeds.

# [REQUIRED FOR UI SAVE, SECRET] Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Keep every old version while a database row still references it.
NAV_CREDENTIAL_ACTIVE_KEY_VERSION=1
NAV_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY_V1=

# [REQUIRED* ENV FALLBACK, SECRET] Required until credentials are saved in UI.
NAV_TECHNICAL_USER_LOGIN=
NAV_TECHNICAL_USER_PASSWORD=
# [REQUIRED*] Your own company's 8-digit tax number (törzsszám) that owns the
# technical user above. Sensitive but not a credential in the same sense as
# the password/sign key; still avoid logging it.
NAV_TECHNICAL_USER_TAX_NUMBER=
# [REQUIRED*, SECRET]
NAV_TECHNICAL_USER_SIGN_KEY=

# [REQUIRED*] Your own software's registered identifier and developer contact
# details with NAV — required by their API contract whenever any NAV feature
# is used, not really "secret" but must be exactly correct.
NAV_SOFTWARE_ID=
NAV_SOFTWARE_DEV_NAME=
NAV_SOFTWARE_DEV_CONTACT=
NAV_SOFTWARE_DEV_TAX_NUMBER=

# [OPTIONAL] Sensible defaults already in code.
NAV_SOFTWARE_NAME=Acropora OS
NAV_SOFTWARE_VERSION=1.0
NAV_SOFTWARE_DEV_COUNTRY_CODE=HU

# [OPTIONAL] Defaults to NAV's live production endpoint. Override only to
# point at NAV's public test system:
#   NAV_API_URL=https://api-test.onlineszamla.nav.gov.hu/invoiceService/v3
NAV_API_URL=https://api.onlineszamla.nav.gov.hu/invoiceService/v3

# [OPTIONAL] Domestic incoming-invoice sync scheduler. Same single-replica
# caveat as the UNAS schedulers above. This note used to say these were
# missing from the development .env.example; re-measured 2026-09-04, all three
# stand in both templates.
NAV_INVOICE_SYNC_ENABLED=false
NAV_INVOICE_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
NAV_INVOICE_SYNC_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=30

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gmail / Foxpost accounting settlement import
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] In-process poller; defaults to disabled. Keep the API at one
# replica while enabled, like the UNAS/NAV schedulers. Manual sync remains
# available from Pénzügy / Foxpost elszámolás even when this flag is false.
GMAIL_FOXPOST_SYNC_ENABLED=false
# [REQUIRED*, SECRET] Google OAuth 2.0 refresh-token credentials for the
# gmail.readonly scope. Required for both automatic and manual Gmail sync.
GMAIL_FOXPOST_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_FOXPOST_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_FOXPOST_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# [OPTIONAL] The mailbox being read. Defaults to info@acropora.hu.
GMAIL_FOXPOST_USER=info@acropora.hu
# [OPTIONAL] Search window is deliberately overlapping; database uniqueness
# on Gmail message id, both attachment hashes and Foxpost settlement identity
# makes re-reading safe and idempotent.
GMAIL_FOXPOST_QUERY=has:attachment filename:xlsx filename:pdf newer_than:90d
GMAIL_FOXPOST_MAX_PAGES=5
GMAIL_FOXPOST_SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
GMAIL_FOXPOST_SYNC_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=60
# [OPTIONAL] Test/mock overrides. Leave empty in production.
GMAIL_API_URL=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Other external API overrides (all optional — safe production defaults exist)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [OPTIONAL] VIES (EU VAT number validation). Override only for a mock/test
# endpoint.
VIES_API_URL=

# [OPTIONAL] MNB (Hungarian National Bank) official exchange rate feed, used
# for purchase-invoice currency conversion. Known limitation, unrelated to
# this configuration: MNB's own bot protection currently blocks this in
# production (see docs/CURRENT_STATUS.md) — manual rate entry is the
# documented workaround until that's resolved upstream.
MNB_API_URL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reserved — not yet wired to any code path
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These exist in the development .env.example as forward-looking placeholders
# for a future real authentication provider (see
# docs/PRODUCTION-DEPLOYMENT-ARCHITECTURE-REVIEW.md, Section 2A) and for the
# not-yet-started Számlázz.hu integration (ADR-005). Grepping the codebase
# confirms zero non-test references today. Leave unset; do not treat their
# presence in the dev example as evidence they're load-bearing.
APP_URL=
SESSION_SECRET=
JWT_SECRET=
SZAMLAZZ_AGENT_KEY=
SZAMLAZZ_API_URL=
SZAMLAZZ_INVOICE_PREFIX=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_PROVIDER — LOAD-BEARING, AND IT MUST STAY EMPTY HERE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This used to sit in the "Reserved" block above, under a comment saying those
# names have zero non-test references and are not load-bearing. That is no
# longer true of this one: the development login (a session without a password,
# which also CREATES an OWNER-role user if none exists) now requires
# AUTH_PROVIDER to be exactly "development". An unset or empty value denies it.
#
# So leaving this line absent or empty is not tidiness, it is the second lock.
# The first is NODE_ENV, and the two are deliberately different variables: a
# copied environment file carries NODE_ENV everywhere, so one lock resting on it
# alone is one lock. See apps/api/src/auth/development-login.guard.ts.
#
# The name is listed here rather than omitted precisely because it appears in
# the development example with a value — someone comparing the two files should
# find the reason here instead of assuming the line was forgotten.
AUTH_PROVIDER=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Medusa (webshop) integration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# None of these stood in either template until 2026-09-04, although the code
# reads nine of them. Whoever configured a deployment could not learn from this
# file what the integration needs.
#
# [REQUIRED*] Admin API base URL of the shop. Not a secret, so it stays in the
# environment rather than in the credential store.
MEDUSA_ADMIN_URL=
#
# [SECRET] [REQUIRED*] THE KEY CAN ARRIVE BY TWO ROUTES, and which one applies
# is decided by the `credentialMode` column of the stored connection row, NOT
# by this file:
#
#   ENV_FALLBACK  -> this variable is required; without it projection stops
#                    with MEDUSA_CONNECTION_NOT_CONFIGURED
#   (stored)      -> the key sits encrypted in the database and is unsealed
#                    with the master key below; this variable is then correctly
#                    EMPTY
#   DISABLED      -> the connection is switched off
#
# Empty is therefore not necessarily a gap: the stored key, entered on the
# Settings page, is the preferred route, because then the secret never lives in
# the process environment.
MEDUSA_ADMIN_API_KEY=
#
# [SECRET] [REQUIRED*] Master key for the stored credential, and the active
# version. THE VERSION NUMBER IS PART OF THE VARIABLE NAME: version 2 is read
# from MEDUSA_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY_V2.
MEDUSA_CREDENTIAL_ACTIVE_KEY_VERSION=1
MEDUSA_CREDENTIAL_MASTER_KEY_V1=
#
# [REQUIRED*] The sales channel a projected product is attached to. Without it
# projection stops on every product -- it does not crash, it states that there
# is nowhere to put the product.
MEDUSA_STOREFRONT_SALES_CHANNEL_ID=

# [OPTIONAL] Projection scheduler. Same single-replica caveat as the UNAS
# schedulers above.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS OFF, AND THAT IS A GUARD, NOT A CONVENIENCE: projection WRITES
# into the shop. Three levels, and the third is not the developers':
#
#   the switch defaults to OFF;
#   it may be turned on in the TEST environment, by the lead's decision;
#   HERE, AGAINST THE LIVE SHOP, TURNING IT ON IS BALAZS'S DECISION ALONE.
#
# That is not a formality: an enabled scheduler writes, on its own, into product
# data the customer sees. Having the right to write is not permission.
#
# Turning it on is setting the variable PLUS restarting the service: the
# scheduler reads its configuration once, at application startup.
MEDUSA_PROJECTION_SCHEDULE_ENABLED=false
# Minutes, between 5 and 1440. A bad value throws AT STARTUP rather than
# silently falling back.
MEDUSA_PROJECTION_SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# Seconds, 0 to 3600: how long the first run waits after startup.
MEDUSA_PROJECTION_SCHEDULE_STARTUP_DELAY_SECONDS=60
# How many products one round projects, 1 to 500. The ceiling is not a
# convenience: an unbounded round would push the whole catalogue at once on a
# first upload.
MEDUSA_PROJECTION_SCHEDULE_BATCH_SIZE=25

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Document store (the master copy of product images)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED*] IF THIS IS MISSING, THE FOLLOWING HAPPENS, AND NO STEP FAILS:
#
#   the image copier and the image publisher each build their OWN in-memory
#     store;
#   the copier writes into one of them, and the row's `storageKey` is updated;
#   the publisher reads from the OTHER one, finds no bytes, and skips the image;
#   projection completes SUCCESSFULLY, the product goes out, just without images.
#
# So its absence does not produce an error, it produces DIFFERENT BEHAVIOUR,
# quietly. Set, the filesystem store is used against this root, and both calls
# get the same store.
DOCUMENT_STORE_ROOT=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Acropora AI
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every behaviour below is read out of the code, including what happens when the
# variable is absent -- none of it is inferred from the name.
#
# [REQUIRED*] [SECRET] The AI service's address and access token. If EITHER is
# missing or blank, aiChatConfig() returns null and the chat layer answers
# `ai_not_configured` -- it names the gap rather than failing obscurely.
ACROPORA_AI_BASE_URL=
ACROPORA_AI_ACCESS_TOKEN=
#
# [REQUIRED*] Record ids (ServiceToken.id) of the dedicated service tokens the
# AI endpoints accept. NOT the tokens themselves: the raw value never enters
# configuration, only its hash reaches the ServiceToken table.
#
# EMPTY MEANS "ACCEPT NOTHING", NEVER "ACCEPT ANYTHING", and the guard's own
# comment says why it is written that way round: an empty allowlist meaning
# "no restriction" would turn a forgotten environment variable into an open
# door onto the whole catalogue, and nothing in the response would show it.
ACROPORA_AI_PRODUCT_SEARCH_TOKEN_ID=
ACROPORA_AI_USER_CONTEXT_TOKEN_ID=
# Same, as a comma-separated LIST. An empty list makes the guard reject with
# "Szolgáltatás-token szükséges."
ACROPORA_CONTENT_AGENT_TOKEN_IDS=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Apple push notifications (APNs)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED*] All four are needed together. If ANY is missing, push is switched
# OFF and the log names what is absent -- and the missing case and the
# unreadable case are two DIFFERENT log lines on purpose: an unconfigured
# system is fine (a developer machine has no key), an unreadable value is not.
#
# [SECRET] The signing key. The code recognises the shape rather than trusting
# the name: the variable says base64, but a PEM pasted in here would otherwise
# be base64-decoded into rubbish under crypto, and the error would be an
# unreadable cryptographic message instead of "the setting is wrong".
APNS_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64=
# [SECRET]
APNS_KEY_ID=
# [SECRET]
APNS_TEAM_ID=
# Not a secret: exactly one of the two words `production` or `sandbox`.
# ANYTHING ELSE IS AN ERROR, NOT A REASON TO PICK A DEFAULT -- silently
# choosing production for a typo would send real notifications from a staging
# deployment.
APNS_ENVIRONMENT=production

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Document store quota
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] WITHOUT THIS THERE IS NO QUOTA, and that is deliberate: an invented
# default limit would one day quietly refuse an upload nobody decided to refuse.
# Unset, zero, or unparseable all mean the same thing -- the check returns
# immediately.
DOCUMENT_STORE_LIMIT_BYTES=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Timeouts
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [OPTIONAL] Node's keep-alive timeout for the API server. Missing or invalid
# falls back to 65000 ms. The server's headersTimeout is kept 5000 ms ABOVE
# whatever this is, so it can never fire on a merely idle connection.
API_KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS=
# [OPTIONAL] The Next.js rewrite proxy's timeout, 1000..600000, default 50000.
# An out-of-range value FALLS BACK rather than throwing, and the code explains
# why: next.config.ts runs during the image build, and a typo that stops the
# build fails the deploy for a reason that looks unrelated to the number.
NEXT_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Release evidence
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED*] The commit SHA of the running build. WITHOUT IT NO EVIDENCE
# LOOKUP IS EVEN POSSIBLE, and the diagnostics say so in words: "A futó build
# nem ismeri a saját commit SHA-ját, ezért semmilyen release evidence nem
# köthető hozzá."
RELEASE_COMMIT_SHA=
# [OPTIONAL] Which repository counts as ours when evidence is checked. Unset
# falls back to KratoBal/acropora-os.
RELEASE_EVIDENCE_EXPECTED_REPOSITORY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development/CI only — must NEVER be set in production
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [DEV ONLY] Opt-in flags for slow integration test suites that hit a real
# database. Not read by any runtime application code, only by test files.
# Do not set these in any production or production-adjacent environment.
# RUN_DB_INTEGRATION=1
# RUN_BRAND_INTEGRATION=1
#
# [CI ONLY] Read by the record-release-evidence database script, NOT by the
# running application -- which is why it stands here rather than above. Absent,
# the detail field is simply left off; PRESENT BUT NOT VALID JSON, the script
# THROWS. That asymmetry is the reason it is worth a line at all.
# RELEASE_EVIDENCE_RESULT_DETAIL_JSON={}
